>
  <article class="body">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>F&#180; components</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Layer 1 as components for NASA JPL&#39;s F&#180; framework, version 4.4.1. Every decision comes from the{' '}
      <a href="/core/quorum-crate/">
        <code>quorum</code> crate
      </a>
      , through a C interface that Kani proves equal to it.
    </p>
    <div class="row" style="gap: 8px; margin: 8px 0 24px">
      <span class="pill">F&#180; v4.4.1</span>
      <span class="pill">C++14</span>
      <span class="pill sig">
        <span class="dot"></span>59 unit tests
      </span>
      <span class="pill">v0.9.0</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      The source is in <a href={`${repo}/fprime`}>fprime/</a> of mruspace/flight. The requirements and the design were
      written and approved in F&#180;&#39;s own order, requirements first, then the model, then the code:{' '}
      <a href={`${docs}/fprime-requirements.md`}>requirements</a>,{' '}
      <a href={`${docs}/fprime-design-fpp.md`}>design and results</a>. The{' '}
      <a href={`${docs}/fault-model.md`}>fault model</a> maps each simulated fault to the failure it stands for on a
      real system.
    </p>

    <h2 id="components">Components</h2>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Component</th>
            <th>Kind</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="hl">
            <td>
              <code>RedundancyManager</code>
            </td>
            <td>active</td>
            <td>
              Asks the replicas for a result each cycle, decides with the core, delivers or rejects, steps the mode down
              as replicas are lost, runs known-answer tests, and resets a replica that falls silent.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>HealthRegistry</code>
            </td>
            <td>passive</td>
            <td>Holds the strike record per replica: three outvotes within 200 cycles start a diagnosis.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>ReplicaHost</code>
            </td>
            <td>active, one per replica</td>
            <td>Runs the payload and answers requests and known-answer tests. A disabled host does not answer.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>SensorVoter</code>
            </td>
            <td>passive</td>
            <td>
              Chooses one value each cycle from up to three readings of one quantity, within a tolerance, and excludes a
              sensor that keeps disagreeing (MRU-SV-001 to 007).
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>FaultInjector</code>
            </td>
            <td>passive, test builds only</td>
            <td>
              Sits between the manager and the replicas in a test topology: silence (kill, hang), a stuck value, and bit
              flips in a replica&#39;s working memory, with the same random upsets as the demo for a seed. A reset on{' '}
              <code>resetIn</code> ends a hang that has started (MRU-FI-004, event <code>ResetApplied</code>).
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>RunRecorder</code>
            </td>
            <td>passive, test builds only</td>
            <td>
              Stands in for the ground in an experiment: checks every delivered result against the payload computed
              without a fault, counts correct and wrong results, and reads the manager&#39;s last telemetry for the
              summary. The manager never sees it.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <code>SensorSim</code>
            </td>
            <td>passive, test builds only</td>
            <td>
              Sends three noisy readings of a known value, with spike, drift, stuck and drop faults. Scores each chosen
              value against the true value, and a plain average as a baseline (MRU-SS-001 to 005).
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      In a flight topology the manager connects to the replicas directly, so no test code is in the flight path.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      In <code>MruOpsSat</code>, the first deployment, the replicas run as three threads of one process. Since v0.7.0,{' '}
      <code>MruQuorum</code> runs each replica as its own <code>MruReplica</code> process. The processes link to the
      manager through F&#180;&#39;s GenericHub over a <code>SOCK_SEQPACKET</code> socket pair, driven by the{' '}
      <code>SeqPacketDriver</code> component. That deployment flew in run 3 on October 28, 2026. Both use the same
      components.
    </p>

    <h2 id="manager">RedundancyManager</h2>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Element</th>
            <th>Name</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Input ports</td>
            <td>
              <code>schedIn</code> (rate group, once per cycle), <code>replyIn[3]</code>, <code>pingIn</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Output ports</td>
            <td>
              <code>requestOut[3]</code>, <code>enableOut[3]</code>, <code>resetOut[3]</code>, <code>resultOut</code>,{' '}
              <code>healthStrike</code>, <code>healthClear</code>, <code>pingOut</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Commands</td>
            <td>
              <code>SET_POLICY</code>, <code>STANDBY_REPLICA</code>, <code>ADMIT_REPLICA</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Parameters</td>
            <td>
              <code>KatPeriod</code> (U32, 64 cycles), <code>StateSavePeriod</code> (U32, 1,000 cycles; 0 saves on
              change only), <code>ResetLimit</code> (U8, default 0: automatic resets off)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Telemetry</td>
            <td>
              <code>CurrentPolicy</code>, <code>CurrentMode</code>, <code>LiveReplicas</code>, <code>Retired</code>,{' '}
              <code>Slots</code>, <code>Resets</code> on change; <code>Delivered</code>, <code>Caught</code>,{' '}
              <code>Late</code> each cycle
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Events</td>
            <td>
              Mode changes, caught disagreements (throttled), diagnoses, retirements with their reason, standby and
              admission, withheld cycles (<code>CycleWithheld</code>), automatic resets (<code>ReplicaReset</code>,{' '}
              <code>ResetsExhausted</code>), the state file, and the halt
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">The full model: RedundancyManager.fpp in fprime/Mru/Components/RedundancyManager.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      A replica is retired when its known-answer result is wrong or missing, or after three missed cycles in a row. The
      event gives the reason: a diagnosis that failed or was not answered, a periodic test on the last replica that
      failed or was not answered, or lost. With <code>ResetLimit</code> above 0, a replica retired for silence gets an
      automatic reset (<a href="#auto-reset">below</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      F&#180; returns 0 for a parameter that was never loaded. The manager then uses the model&#39;s defaults, so a
      topology that skips <code>loadParameters()</code> does not run the known-answer test every cycle.
    </p>

    <h2 id="slots">Spares and standby</h2>
    <p>
      Each of the three slots is ACTIVE, STANDBY or RETIRED. Only ACTIVE slots get requests. These follow section 8 of
      the <a href="https://mru.space/mru-whitepaper.pdf">whitepaper</a>, where salvaged processors join as spares and
      declining nodes are set aside.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>STANDBY_REPLICA</strong> disables an ACTIVE slot to save power. It is refused if the policy could not
        run on the slots left. A power accountant will use it; that accountant is not built.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ADMIT_REPLICA</strong> enables a STANDBY slot, or new hardware in a RETIRED slot, and asks for its known
        answer. The slot becomes ACTIVE only if the right answer arrives by the end of the next cycle.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="auto-reset">Automatic resets</h2>
    <p>
      A replica can fall silent for a reason that a reset cures: for example, a device on a bus that stops answering
      until it is reset. Since v0.8.0 the manager handles this case (MRU-RM-024). It is off by default:{' '}
      <code>ResetLimit</code> is 0.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Reset, then test.</strong> When a replica is retired for silence (lost, or its known answer missing),
        the manager sends a reset on <code>resetOut</code> and emits <code>ReplicaReset</code>. At the next cycle it
        starts an admission, the same as <code>ADMIT_REPLICA</code>&#39;s. The slot becomes ACTIVE again only on the
        right known answer. Admissions after resets wait behind an admission in progress.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bounded.</strong> A unit still silent gets the next reset. After <code>ResetLimit</code> resets the
        manager emits <code>ResetsExhausted</code> and the slot stays RETIRED, so a dead unit cannot cause a reset loop.
        The limit counts every reset of the slot since the manager started. An admission that passes does not clear it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never after a wrong answer.</strong> A wrong answer is not silence. A replica retired for a wrong answer
        is never reset.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In the runs, <code>resetOut</code> goes to <code>FaultInjector</code>. There a reset ends a hang that has started.
      A hang scheduled for later still comes, and a kill or a stuck value stays. On a real system, <code>resetOut</code>{' '}
      would drive the platform&#39;s reset line for that unit. Mru decides when to reset and whether to take the unit
      back. It does not reset hardware itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="state">State across resets</h2>
    <p>
      The manager saves the policy, the slot states and the cycle counter when one of them changes, and every{' '}
      <code>StateSavePeriod</code> cycles. The record is 21 bytes with a CRC-32. It writes a new file, flushes it and
      renames it over the old one, so a power cut leaves the old state or the new one. At start it restores a valid file
      and reports it, or starts with all slots ACTIVE and warns. Strikes are not saved: after a reset each replica
      starts a fresh window. Automatic reset counts are not saved either. After the manager restarts, each slot starts
      again with no resets used.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sensors">SensorVoter and SensorSim</h2>
    <p>
      Readings of one quantity by three sensors never match exactly. Noise, calibration and timing make them differ a
      little, so the replicas&#39; exact vote does not fit them. <code>SensorVoter</code> compares readings within a
      tolerance instead. It is a flight component, added in v0.8.0. It makes no decision itself: it calls{' '}
      <code>quorum_sensor_select</code> and <code>quorum_sensor_observe</code> (MRU-SV-001).
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Element</th>
            <th>Name</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Input port</td>
            <td>
              <code>readingsIn</code> (cycle, three <code>I32</code> values, three presence flags)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Output port</td>
            <td>
              <code>selectedOut</code> (cycle, value, checked)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Command</td>
            <td>
              <code>INCLUDE_SENSOR</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Parameters</td>
            <td>
              <code>Tolerance</code> (U32, default 0, in the readings&#39; units), <code>ExcludeAfter</code> (U8,
              default 5; 0 never excludes)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Telemetry</td>
            <td>
              <code>Agreed</code>, <code>Unchecked</code>, <code>Disagreed</code>, <code>Outliers</code> each cycle;{' '}
              <code>Excluded</code> on change
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Events</td>
            <td>
              <code>SensorOutlier</code> and <code>SensorsDisagree</code> (both throttled at 10),{' '}
              <code>UncheckedValue</code> (throttled at 5), <code>SensorExcluded</code>, <code>SensorIncluded</code>,{' '}
              <code>IncludeRefused</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">The full model: SensorVoter.fpp in fprime/Mru/Components/SensorVoter.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      Each cycle the voter marks the readings of excluded sensors absent and chooses from the rest:
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Three readings:</strong> the median, if at least one other reading is within the tolerance of it. A
        reading further away is an outlier, with an event, and is never chosen. If no other reading is that close, the
        readings disagree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two readings:</strong> their midpoint, if they are within the tolerance of each other. If not, nothing
        says which is right, and the voter delivers nothing for the cycle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One reading:</strong> delivered, marked unchecked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Exclusion:</strong> a sensor that is an outlier in <code>ExcludeAfter</code> agreed cycles in a row is
        excluded, and its readings are no longer used. A short spike is forgiven. A cycle with no agreed value changes
        no count. <code>INCLUDE_SENSOR</code> brings an excluded sensor back with its count at 0.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <code>SensorSim</code> is for test builds only. After <code>SENSORS_ON(noise, accuracy, seed)</code> it sends
      three readings of a known value each cycle: a triangle wave from 20,000 to 22,000 and back every 400 cycles, with
      seeded uniform noise. <code>SENSOR_FAULT</code> schedules a <code>SPIKE</code>, <code>DRIFT</code>,{' '}
      <code>STUCK</code> or <code>DROP</code> on one sensor. It counts correct, wrong and unchecked values, cycles with
      no value, and cycles in which a plain average of the readings was wrong (<code>Correct</code>, <code>Wrong</code>,{' '}
      <code>UncheckedValues</code>, <code>Withheld</code>, <code>AverageWrong</code>). Both components are in{' '}
      <code>MruQuorum</code>, off until <code>SENSORS_ON</code>. When on, the summary adds a <code>sensors:</code> line.
    </p>
    <p>
      Run 4, segment 4 runs them for 40 s: a spike on sensor 1 at cycle 60 (three cycles), a drift on sensor 2 from
      cycle 120, and sensor 0 frozen from cycle 260. Tolerance 2.00, noise up to 0.50 (the readings are in hundredths),
      exclusion after 5 outlier cycles. In orbit in run 4, on November 20, 2026, and in a full-length native run in the
      build container, 399 cycles gave 279 correct values, 0 wrong, and 120 cycles with no value. The spike was masked
      and sensor 1 kept. Sensor 2 was excluded at cycle 137. From cycle 279 the two readings left disagreed, so the
      voter delivered nothing. A plain average of the same readings was wrong in 261 of the 399 cycles. Source: the{' '}
      <a href={`${docs}/fault-model.md#run-4-flown-november-20-2026`}>fault model</a>. The readings are simulated, with
      a known true value.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cycle">One cycle</h2>
    <p>The order follows a tick of the demo, so the same seed gives the same counts:</p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Close the last cycle.</strong> Decide it if it is not yet decided, with a missing reply as absent. Count
        missed replies, and fail tests that had a full cycle to answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Start the next.</strong> Ask every ACTIVE slot. On the last replica, ask for two results on even cycles
        only, and add the known-answer test every <code>KatPeriod</code> cycles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decide as soon as every answer is in.</strong> Strikes, the test of two disagreeing replicas, and the
        periodic test follow at once.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A replica that dies stops answering, so the manager finds the death by silence. When a death leaves one replica,
      the cycles until it is marked lost end as caught disagreements. The demo kills its replicas itself and knows at
      once; this is the one place the counts differ.
    </p>

    <h2 id="deployment">Deployment</h2>
    <p>
      <code>MruOpsSat</code> is the first F&#180; deployment of the components. It runs them with F&#180;&#39;s own
      services: the command dispatcher, the event manager, the telemetry database, health pings and a command sequencer
      (the <code>CdhCore</code> subtopology), driven by a Linux timer every 100 ms. The faults and the policy come from
      F&#180; command sequences, as an operator would send them. It has no communications stack: events are written as
      text lines and the summary as a file.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is built as a static, optimized 32-bit ARM binary for the SEPP, the Cortex-A9 payload processor of OPS-SAT
      PRETTY. In ESA&#39;s OPS-SAT experiment environment, on a laptop under emulation, the package ran full length on
      October 22, 2026: 148 seconds, 0 wrong results, the counts of the demo within the system test&#39;s rule, a
      restore from the state file after a kill, and 1.2% to 1.5% CPU in the timed run. The second deployment flew in its
      place. See <a href={`${docs}/opssat.md`}>the operations note</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A second deployment puts each replica in a process of its own: <code>MruQuorum</code>, the manager process, starts
      three <code>MruReplica</code> processes, linked through F&#180;&#39;s GenericHub over <code>SOCK_SEQPACKET</code>{' '}
      Unix socket pairs (no network), and pins them to the SEPP&#39;s two cores. The kill is real: the manager sends
      SIGKILL to a replica process at the start of a cycle. In ESA&#39;s environment under emulation it gave the same
      counts and events as the first deployment, with the three processes pinned in every segment. It ran on the SEPP,
      on the FlatSat and in orbit, on October 28, 2026 (<a href="#opssat-run3">below</a>). Both deployments wire{' '}
      <code>manager.resetOut</code> to <code>injector.resetIn</code>. Only <code>MruQuorum</code> holds{' '}
      <code>SensorVoter</code> and <code>SensorSim</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      On one processor the manager and the replicas share the processor. A manager that falls behind used to close
      cycles before their answers were dispatched, so healthy replicas looked silent and were retired as lost. Since
      v0.6.0 a cycle that closes late, with other messages still in the manager&#39;s queue or every expected answer
      missing at once, counts against no replica; with an answer missing it is withheld and counted (MRU-RM-023). In a
      container starved of processor time, the old rule halted 9 of 15 runs and the new one none; no run gave a wrong
      result.
    </p>

    <h2 id="links">Message check</h2>
    <p>
      In <code>MruQuorum</code> every reply crosses a socket. Since v0.9.0 the manager process checks each message from
      a replica on its link, before the hub decodes it (MRU-DEP-008). A message passes only if it is exactly one
      well-formed <code>ReplicaReply</code> on hub port 0: the right type, port and length, a valid{' '}
      <code>RequestKind</code>, and nothing left over. The manager takes at most four messages from a replica in one
      cycle. It drops and counts anything else. The summary gives the count per replica on its{' '}
      <code>links: dropped=</code> line. A replica that sends only garbage is silent to the manager, and is lost.
    </p>
    <p>
      Real bit flips made by software found the need for this check (MRU-DEP-009), in the package for run 4, segment 6.
      A corrupted replica sent a reply too short to decode. F&#180;&#39;s generated port code in the manager asserted on
      it and ended the manager process, in 1 of 64 native runs at 200 flips per second from start-up. After the fix, the
      manager did not crash in 160 native runs. See the <a href={`${docs}/fault-model.md`}>fault model</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="opssat-run3">On OPS-SAT</h2>
    <p>
      The second deployment ran in orbit on ESA&#39;s OPS-SAT PRETTY satellite on October 28, 2026, at 09:13 UTC, as run
      3 of experiment exp4051, after ESA&#39;s software environment and the TU Graz FlatSat the same morning. It was the
      first run of these components on flight hardware: the SEPP, with each replica process pinned to a core (0, 1, 1).
      Three segments in 148 s, the faults of run 1 from an F&#180; command sequence. Segment 1: the shrinking quorum; a
      bit flip at cycle 60 outvoted, replica 1 stuck at 120 and retired at 122, replica 0&#39;s process killed with
      SIGKILL at the start of cycle 240 and retired as lost at 242, then self-check on the last replica from 243.
      Segment 2: fixed TMR on the same faults. Segment 3: the shrinking quorum with replica 1 retired at 202; the
      manager killed 35 s in and started again, restored from its state file at cycle 303 with replica 1 still retired.
      Results matched the prediction written before the run.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Run 3</th>
            <th>1 · Shrinking quorum</th>
            <th>2 · Fixed TMR</th>
            <th>3 · Restart</th>
            <th>Total</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>FlatSat, 2026-10-28</td>
            <td class="m">342 correct, 3 caught, 0 wrong, 1 withheld</td>
            <td class="m">239 correct, caught to cycle 348, 1 withheld</td>
            <td class="m">299 correct, 0 wrong, restored at 303</td>
            <td class="m">148 s</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>In orbit, 2026-10-28</td>
            <td class="m">342 correct, 3 caught, 0 wrong</td>
            <td class="m">239 correct, caught to cycle 348</td>
            <td class="m">299 correct, 0 wrong, restored at 303</td>
            <td class="m">148 s</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny">
      From the summary, event and state files and run-info.txt in the
      <a href="https://github.com/mruspace/flight/tree/main/opssat/results/2026-10-28-ops-sat-pretty-on-board-run3">
        result files
      </a>
      . On the FlatSat the kill landed while the manager was behind, so it withheld cycle 240 (MRU-RM-023) and retired
      replica 0 one cycle later; the counts did not change. In orbit no cycle was withheld. On the SEPP the manager used
      1.7 MB resident and 11.8 MB of address space, and 0.40% to 0.46% of wall time; all four processes together 0.79%
      to 0.96%. Scope: one payload processor with two cores, in user space, faults from a command sequence; not a
      hardware-redundancy test. Write-up:
      <a href="https://mru.space/research/flight-orbit-3/">№ 008</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="opssat-run4">Run 4</h2>
    <p>
      Run 4 was approved by the OPS-SAT team on November 16, 2026. The v0.9.0 package ran on TU Graz&#39;s FlatSat and
      in orbit on OPS-SAT PRETTY on November 20, 2026: 244 seconds in orbit, every exit 0, 0 wrong results in every
      segment. It runs <code>MruQuorum</code> with three <code>MruReplica</code> processes and the cFS app, in six
      segments of 40 s, at 100 ms per cycle. Segments 1 to 3 share their faults: replica 1 hangs at cycle 60 and again
      at 300, and replica 2&#39;s process is killed with SIGKILL at cycle 150, a real dead unit.
    </p>
