    <p class="tiny">
      Source: the files the satellite sent down, in{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/mruspace/flight/tree/main/opssat/results/2026-11-20-ops-sat-pretty-on-board-run4">
        opssat/results
      </a>
      , and the <a href={`${docs}/fault-model.md#run-4-flown-november-20-2026`}>fault model</a>. On the FlatSat the same
      morning every count was the same, except where a cycle closed late and the manager withheld it (MRU-RM-023):
      replica 1 back at 304, and fixed TMR 149 correct. Replicas 0 and 1 of segment 6 crashed at shutdown, after the
      last result. Before flight, in ESA&#39;s environment image (32-bit ARM emulated on a laptop), three full runs took
      246 s each, every exit 0, 0 wrong in every segment. Faults are simulated. In segment 6 they are real bit flips
      made by software, in user space, one bit at a time. The runs show the recovery logic, not a recovered device: a
      reset ends a simulated hang.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      In segment 6 a thread in each replica process flips one random bit at a time in that process&#39;s own memory: its
      code, read-only data, data, heap and stacks. It logs each flip before it makes it. The manager runs the shrinking
      quorum with no resets and a known-answer test on the last replica every cycle, and is told nothing about the
      flips. A flipped replica stops at 2 s of CPU time and 256 KB of files, and no process writes a core file.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cfs">cFS application</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={`${repo}/cfs`}>cfs/</a> runs the same decision core as an application of NASA&#39;s core Flight System
      (cFS), added in v0.8.0. cFE, OSAL and PSP are pinned at the commits of the cFS bundle v7.0.1, unchanged. The Mru
      app is C99. It runs three replicas as cFE child tasks, with the payload of the F&#180; deployment, and takes every
      decision through <code>quorum.h</code>. Faults come from a schedule in <code>/cf/mru.cfg</code>. No sample or lab
      application is built, so nothing opens a network socket.
    </p>
    <p>
      Around the core the app keeps the F&#180; manager&#39;s rules: strikes, known-answer tests, loss after three
      silent cycles, self-check on the last replica, and a withheld cycle when every answer is missing. It does not have
      the automatic resets or the sensor voter: those are F&#180; only. <code>cfs/build.sh</code> builds it in the
      F&#180; build container, static 32-bit ARM by default and native with <code>NATIVE=1</code>. CI builds it
      natively.
    </p>
    <p>
      Natively, under an enforced 128 MB address-space limit with <code>MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=1</code>, the process used 9.0
      MB of address space (November 13, 2026). In ESA&#39;s OPS-SAT environment image on November 13, 2026, run 4
      segment 5 ran 40 s at 100 ms with the faults of run 1: 400 cycles, 318 correct, 3 caught, 0 wrong. The stuck
      replica 1 was retired on its known answer at cycle 122, and the hung replica 0 was lost at cycle 242. No socket
      was open, and nothing was left behind. Source: <a href={`${repo}/cfs/README.md`}>cfs/README.md</a>. On November
      20, 2026, it ran in orbit on OPS-SAT PRETTY in run 4 with the same counts, its first run on flight hardware, and
      peaked at 4.5 MB of address space on the SEPP.
    </p>

    <h2 id="c">C interface</h2>
    <p>
      <code>quorum-ffi</code> builds the core as a <code>no_std</code> static library with a C header,{' '}
      <a href={`${repo}/quorum-ffi/include/quorum.h`}>quorum.h</a>. It is not tied to F&#180;: any C or C++ framework
      can use it. The cFS app uses it too.
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">uint8_t quorum_mode(uint8_t policy, uint32_t alive);
quorum_verdict quorum_decide(uint8_t policy, const quorum_reply *replies);
void quorum_health_init(quorum_health *health);
bool quorum_health_strike(quorum_health *health, uint32_t replica, uint64_t cycle);
void quorum_health_clear(quorum_health *health, uint32_t replica);
quorum_sensor_selection quorum_sensor_select(const quorum_reading *readings, uint32_t tolerance);
quorum_sensor_run quorum_sensor_observe(uint8_t run, bool outlier, uint8_t limit);</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">The sensor functions use these constants and types:</p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">/* sensors (quorum::sensor) */
#define QUORUM_SENSORS 3
#define QUORUM_SENSOR_AGREED 0    /* two or more readings agree within the tolerance */
#define QUORUM_SENSOR_UNCHECKED 1 /* the only reading, not cross-checked */
#define QUORUM_SENSOR_DISAGREE 2  /* readings disagree; nothing delivered */
#define QUORUM_SENSOR_NOTHING 3   /* no reading */

/* One sensor&#39;s reading; present is false for no reading or a sensor excluded. */
typedef struct {'{'}
    bool present;
    int32_t value;
{'}'} quorum_reading;

/* The choice for one cycle. */
typedef struct {'{'}
    uint8_t outcome;                  /* AGREED, UNCHECKED, DISAGREE or NOTHING */
    int32_t value;                    /* meaningful only for AGREED and UNCHECKED */
    bool outlier[QUORUM_SENSORS];     /* readings beyond the tolerance (three readings, AGREED) */
{'}'} quorum_sensor_selection;

/* A sensor&#39;s outlier count after one cycle. */
typedef struct {'{'}
    uint8_t run;  /* outlier cycles in a row */
    bool exclude; /* the count reached the limit: exclude the sensor */
{'}'} quorum_sensor_run;</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      Only plain values cross. The health record lives in the caller&#39;s memory. Out-of-range input gets the safe
      answer: a policy that is not 0 or 1 halts, an unknown reply kind counts as absent, a replica above 2 changes
      nothing, and a null pointer does the same. Seven Kani proofs show that <code>quorum_mode</code>,{' '}
      <code>quorum_decide</code>, <code>quorum_health_strike</code>, <code>quorum_health_clear</code>,{' '}
      <code>quorum_sensor_select</code> and <code>quorum_sensor_observe</code> give the core&#39;s answer for every
      valid input, and the safe answer for a bad policy, an unknown reply kind or a replica above 2. Unit tests check
      the null pointers. See <a href="/core/quorum-crate/#proofs">Kani proofs</a>.
    </p>

