      The build calls <code>cargo</code> to compile the core, so it needs a Rust toolchain. On macOS 27, the CMake that
      F&#180; v4.4.1 pins fails; CMake 4.4 works. Linux works with the pinned version.
    </p>

    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Two runs on flight hardware.</strong> The second deployment ran twice in orbit, 148 and 244 seconds on
        one payload processor with two cores, in user space, with faults from a command sequence and, in run 4, real bit
        flips made by software. Not a hardware-redundancy test and not a radiation test. Everything else runs in tests
        on general-purpose processors, the ARM build under emulation. See the{' '}
        <a href="/concepts/architecture/">architecture status</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Simulated faults.</strong> The runs reproduce the symptoms of hardware faults, not their causes. The one
        exception is run 4, segment 6: real bit flips made by software, in user space, one bit at a time. The resets end
        a simulated hang, so the runs show the recovery logic, not a recovered device. The readings of{' '}
        <code>SensorSim</code> are simulated. See the <a href={`${docs}/fault-model.md#not-modeled`}>fault model</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No ground link yet.</strong> The first deployment has no communications stack. A deployment with
        commands and telemetry from the ground is planned.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not built:</strong> the retry of a cycle after a disagreement on two replicas (the whitepaper&#39;s
        &quot;dual with checkpointing&quot;), a step that makes every replica&#39;s input equal before it computes, the
        health score by error rate and response time, the power accountant, and memory scrubbing.
      </li>
    </ul>
